# Extension shell

WXT drives the build with `srcDir: "src/extension"`, so entrypoint **file names are meaningful** — see
[wxt.config.ts](mdc:wxt.config.ts). The manifest is generated there; don't add a static `manifest.json`.

## Entrypoint kinds

| Pattern | WXT API | Notes |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `*.content.ts` | `defineContentScript` | Runs in the isolated world on matched Torn pages. `extension.content.ts` is the main one — `document_start`, all of torn.com minus logout/api/wiki. |
| `*--inject.ts` | `defineUnlistedScript` | Main-world script, injected on demand. |
| `background/index.ts` | `defineBackground` | The service worker. |
| `<page>/index.html` + `<page>.ts` | — | Extension pages: options, popup, targets, tos, offscreen. The `.ts` just mounts a root Svelte component. |
| `service-worker.ts` | `defineUnlistedScript` | Notification-click fallback for Android. |

Extension pages follow one shape:

```ts
import { findElement } from "@common/utils/functions/find-elements.ts";
import "@svelte/app.css";
import { mount } from "svelte";
import Popup from "./Popup.svelte";

mount(Popup, { target: findElement("#app") });
```

## The `--inject` convention

Content scripts run in an isolated world and cannot see page globals — `window.fetch` as the page sees it, jQuery,
`torn.model`. Reaching those requires a script running in the page's own world, and the `--inject` suffix is what makes
that work: it matches the `*--inject.js` glob already listed under `web_accessible_resources`, so **a new inject script
needs no manifest change**.

```ts
// src/extension/entrypoints/my-thing--inject.ts
import { setRuntimeInformation } from "@common/utils/context";
import { DEFAULT_RUNTIME_INFORMATION } from "@common/utils/functions/context-interfaces";

export default defineUnlistedScript(() => {
	setRuntimeInformation(DEFAULT_RUNTIME_INFORMATION);
	// page-world work
});
```

Inject it from the content script side:

```ts
import { executeScript } from "@common/utils/functions/dom";
import { browser } from "wxt/browser";

executeScript(browser.runtime.getURL("/my-thing--inject.js"), false, true);   // (url, remove, unique)
```

Rules of thumb:

- The two worlds share no variables. Communicate with `CustomEvent` — see `information-retriever--inject.ts` paired with
  `extension-information-retriever.ts`, and the `EVENT_CHANNELS` helpers in `@common/utils/functions/events`.
- Inject scripts get no extension APIs and no storage. Keep them minimal: observe or expose something, post it back, let
  the content script do the rest.
- Broadly reusable injections belong on `ExtensionScriptInjector` in `src/extension/runtime/extension-context.ts`
  (`injectFetch`, `injectXHR`, ...), which guards against double injection. Only inject directly from a feature for
  one-off cases.
- Pass `unique: true` when the script must not run twice on one page.

## Background

`background/index.ts` registers listeners and services; the work itself lives in siblings:

- `updates/` — the periodic data fetchers, orchestrated by `timedUpdates()`.
- `notifications.ts` — dispatch, sound and TTS via the offscreen document, plus history cleanup.
- `updates-helper.ts` — shared helpers (and one of the few unit-tested spots).

Alarms are the scheduling mechanism (`data-update-alarm` every 30s, cache clearing and notification cleanup hourly),
created in `resetAlarms()`. A service worker can be torn down at any time, so keep no meaningful in-memory state: read
from storage, write back through `ttStorage`.

## Proxy services

UI and content scripts reach the background through `@webext-core/proxy-service`, never with hand-rolled
`runtime.sendMessage`:

```ts
import { BACKGROUND_SERVICE } from "@extension/services/proxy-services";

await BACKGROUND_SERVICE.forceUpdateUserdata();
```

`BackgroundService` covers notifications, sound tests, `fetchRelay`, forced data updates, cache operations and alarm
resets; `SourceService` resolves stack traces through source maps. To add a capability, put the method on the service
class — it is instantly available through the proxy on the other side.

## Cross-browser notes

- Builds target Chrome/Edge (MV3) and Firefox (MV3, `strict_min_version` 128). `background` and `offscreen` permissions
  are Chrome-only and already branched on `browser` in the manifest function.
- Firefox needs `wrappedJSObject` in places and parses stack traces differently; there is existing handling for both —
  match it rather than special-casing inline.
- Cross-origin requests from content scripts must relay through the background (`relay: true` in `fetchData`).
- After changing entrypoints, the manifest or `wxt.config.ts`, run `bun run build` — those breakages don't show up in
  `bun run types`.
